# Topic permissions per authenticated user. No secrets live here - only the
# password file (generated at container startup, never committed) does.
# See docs/03-mqtt-layer.md for the full topic contract and the reasoning
# behind this design.

# --- backend: fleet-wide visibility, can command any robot ---
# Explicitly NOT granted write on telemetry/health/status/heartbeat - a
# compromised backend should not be able to impersonate a robot's own
# reported state, only issue commands and read what robots report.
user backend
topic readwrite robots/+/cmd
topic read robots/+/telemetry
topic read robots/+/health
topic read robots/+/status
topic read robots/+/camera
topic read robots/+/heartbeat

# --- robots: one rule set for every robot, no per-robot editing ---
# Each robot's MQTT username IS its robot_id (see
# docker-entrypoint-wrapper.sh), so Mosquitto's %u substitution scopes every
# robot to exactly its own robots/{robot_id}/... namespace automatically.
# Onboarding robot #2 means issuing it a credential, not editing this file -
# the same role AWS IoT Core policy variables like
# ${iot:Connection.Thing.ThingName} play for per-thing policies.
pattern read robots/%u/cmd
pattern write robots/%u/telemetry
pattern write robots/%u/health
pattern write robots/%u/status
pattern write robots/%u/camera
pattern write robots/%u/heartbeat
